('127.314.d.4.C',4,'In SAMR, the highest level, where technology allows tasks not possible before, is:','["augmentation", "modification", "substitution", "redefinition"]'::jsonb,3,'Redefinition transforms learning.','original'),
('127.314.d.4.D',3,'Before using a new app with students, a teacher should usually:','["get it approved by the district", "use it secretly", "download it without checking", "ask students to sign up with personal emails"]'::jsonb,0,'Approval protects privacy.','original'),
('127.314.d.4.D',4,'A district''s acceptable use policy (AUP) explains:','["parking rules", "how technology may and may not be used", "bus schedules", "lunch prices"]'::jsonb,1,'AUPs set technology rules.','original'),
('127.314.d.4.E',3,'To keep students on task during device use, a teacher can:','["allow any site", "leave the room", "set clear expectations and monitor screens", "ignore off-task use"]'::jsonb,2,'Monitoring supports focus.','original'),
('127.314.d.4.E',4,'A routine where devices are closed during instruction helps:','["increase distractions", "remove rules", "reduce learning", "keep students focused on the teacher"]'::jsonb,3,'Clear routines limit distractions.','original'),
